Physical Constants and Conversion Factors
The tables in this chapter supply some of the more commonly needed physical constants and conversion factors.
All scientific measurements are based upon four international arbitrarily adopted units, the magnitudes of which are fixed by four agreed on standards:

Length--the meter--fixed by the vacuum wavelength of radiation corresponding to the transition 2P_{10}-5D_{\mathrm{s}}\) of krypton 86
(1 meter=1650763.73\lambda)
Mass--the kilogram--fixed by the international kilogram at Sevres, France.

Time--the second--fixed as 1/31,556,925.9747 of the tropical year 1900 at 12^{\circ} ephemeris time.

Temperature--the degree--fixed on a thermodynamic basis by taking the temperature for the triple point of natural water as 273.16 degK. (The Celsius scale is obtained by adding -273.15 to the Kelvin scale.)

All other units are defined in terms of them by assigning the value unity to the proportionality constant in each defining equation, the system so derived being called the MKS system. Taking the 1/100 part of the meter as the unit of length and the 1/1000 part of the kilogram as the unit of mass similarly gives rise to the CGS system, often used in physics and chemistry. The more common named units and their conversion factors are given in Table 2.1.
Table 2.2. Names and Conversion Factors for Electric and Magnetic Units
MLKS (Quantity name name name) (MRS unit/ cmunit) (MRS unit/ cmunit)
The practical, or MKSA, electrical units are defined by the force per unit length between two infinitely long parallel filamentary conductors carrying current when unit distance apart in a vacuum by the equation \Gamma_{m}I_{1}I_{2}/4\pi=2F. If F is in newtons and \Gamma_{m} has the numerical value 4\pi\times 10^{-7} then \(I_{1}\) and \(I_{2}\) are measured in terms of the practical unit, the ampere. The customary equations of the rationalized MKSA system then define the other electric and magnetic units. The force between electric charges in a vacuum in this system is given by \(Q_{1}Q_{2}/4\pi\Gamma_{e}r^{2}=F\), \(\Gamma_{e}\) having the numerical value 10'/4\pi c^{2} where e is the speed of light in meters per second (\Gamma_{e}=8.854\times 10^{-12}\)).

The CGS unrationalized system is obtained by deleting 4\(\pi\) in the denominators in these equations and expressing \(F\) in dynes and r in centimeters Setting \(\Gamma_{m}\) equal to unity defines the CGS unrationalized electromagnetic system (emu), \(\Gamma_{e}\) taking the numerical value of 1/e\({}^{2}\). Setting \(\Gamma_{e}\) equal to unity defines the CGS unrationalized electrostatic system (esu), \(\Gamma_{m}\) taking the numerical value of 1/e\({}^{2}\).

The Lorentz-Heaviside system involves a different process of rationalization.

Example: If the value assigned to a current is 100 amperes its value in abamperes is 100\times 10^{-1}=10.
*Divide this number by 4\(\pi\) if unrationalized MKS system is involved; other numbers are unchanged.
